    "bio": "A handy left arm pacer, Gurjapneet Singh was bought by the Super Kings for a sum of INR 2.20 Crores. Gurjapneet has also played in the TNPL, featuring for teams like Madurai Panthers and Dindigul Dragons.",
    "content": "<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Some journeys in cricket feels earned, not engineered. The Gurjapneet Singh bowler&#8217;s story is one of them. It is built on relocation, resilience, and relentless improvement. A left arm fast bowler who chose discomfort over comfort, he left home young to find a stronger cricket pathway. Today, he stands as one of the rare Tamil Nadu left arm seamers pushing for a sustained IPL role in yellow.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">CSK did not retain him out of sentiment. They retained him because left arm pace with discipline and intent is difficult to find. Injuries slowed him recently, but belief inside the setup never dipped. At Chepauk, the excitement is simple. A fresh left armer with the new ball who can shape spells, not just bowl overs.</p>\n\n\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\"><strong>Gurjapneet Singh Personal Details</strong></h3>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><strong>Name:</strong> Gurjapneet Singh</li>\n\n\n\n<li><strong>Born:</strong> November 08, 1998</li>\n\n\n\n<li><strong>Age:</strong> 27y 71d</li>\n\n\n\n<li><strong>Nationality:</strong> India</li>\n\n\n\n<li><strong>Batting Style:</strong> Right-hand bat</li>\n\n\n\n<li><strong>Bowling Style:</strong> Left-arm medium fast</li>\n\n\n\n<li><strong>Playing Role:</strong> Bowler</li>\n</ul>\n\n\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Who is Gurjapneet Singh?</h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><a href=\"/player/gurjapneet-singh/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\"><strong>Gurjapneet Singh </strong></a>is a left arm fast bowler born in Ludhiana and raised in Ambala before reshaping his future in Chennai. He embedded himself in the Tamil Nadu Cricket Association league system and rose through club cricket into state representation.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The Gurjapneet Singh bowler profile stands on control and seam position rather than raw pace. He hits the deck hard, attacks the stumps, and bowls with clarity. In red ball cricket, he has shown stamina and patience. In white ball formats, he has added aggression and smarter angles with the new ball. He is a thinking seamer who understands phases of play.</p>\n\n\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Why Gurjapneet Singh Left Home?</h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">At around 17, Gurjapneet made a decisive move. He struggled to break into Punjab’s age group structure despite steady performances. A coach advised him to shift base. Chennai offered deeper league competition and clearer exposure. Around 2015, he relocated alone and joined local sides like Egmore RC, beginning the long climb through Tamil Nadu cricket.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">His breakthrough arrived in 2021 with Dindigul Dragons in the Tamil Nadu Premier League. Former Tamil Nadu pacer Yo Mahesh guided him through his first high pressure T20 environment and reinforced trust in his strengths. In 2022, a lower back stress reaction sidelined him for seven to eight months. He rebuilt through structured rehab and conditioning. By 2025, the gamble paid off when Chennai Super Kings signed him at the IPL auction, validating a decision made as a teenager with nothing but belief.</p>\n\n\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Gurjapneet Singh Domestic Stats</h2>\n\n\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-table\"><table class=\"has-fixed-layout\"><tbody><tr><td><strong>Format</strong></td><td><strong>Matches</strong></td><td><strong>Wickets</strong></td><td><strong>BBI</strong></td><td><strong>Average</strong></td><td><strong>Economy</strong></td><td><strong>Strike Rate</strong></td><td><strong>4w</strong></td><td><strong>5w</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>FC</strong></td><td>8</td><td><strong>31</strong></td><td>6/22</td><td>20.74</td><td>2.83</td><td>43.9</td><td>4</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td><strong>List A</strong></td><td>9</td><td><strong>17</strong></td><td>6/26</td><td>20.41</td><td>5.33</td><td>22.9</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td><strong>T20s</strong></td><td>15</td><td><strong>21</strong></td><td>3/18</td><td>24.38</td><td>9.25</td><td>15.8</td><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Gurjapneet Singh’s bowling record across formats shows a rare blend of control and penetration for a left-arm seamer. In red-ball cricket, he has already proven he can shoulder responsibility, bowling long spells, attacking the stumps, and producing decisive performances that break games open. His ability to extract movement and maintain discipline stands out, especially for someone still early in his career. </p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">In one-day cricket, he becomes even more threatening, striking regularly and using angles smartly to keep batters guessing. His white-ball growth is evident in T20s as well, where he attacks in short bursts, looks for breakthroughs, and isn’t afraid to take risks with the new ball. The numbers reflect a bowler who doesn’t just contain, he hunts wickets. For us, that profile fits perfectly: a left-armer who brings variation, intent, and genuine impact potential.</p>",
